# Current events in town, part seven: births, apprenticeships, successions and arrests heard in more places (the shore,
# the fields, the chapel, the hall, the barracks), so the rarer news has more than one way to be told.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- Births

scene et_bi_shore
when topic=life kind=birth place=shore
a : {subject}'s pup was born the night the boats came in full. Lucky. | They say {subject}'s pup came with the tide. | A fisher's pup for {subject}. Webbed paws, I bet.
b[fisher] : Born on a full catch. That pup'll never go hungry.
b : Luckiest night there is. | Tide-born, sea-blessed. | Ha. Hope not.
a : The very luckiest. | So they say. | Hope so.

scene et_bi_field
when topic=life kind=birth place=field|work
a : {subject}'s had the pup. Back in the field in a week, I'll bet. | Heard the news in the furrows. {subject}'s pup. | {subject}'s pup came in the middle of the harvest.
b[farmer] : Fields don't wait for pups. Pups don't wait for harvest.
b : A week? Mad. | Good news travels down the rows. | Worst timing.
a : That's {subject}. | It does. | Best timing, really.

scene et_bi_chapel
when topic=life kind=birth place=chapel
a : {subject} brought the pup for blessing this morning. | The priest held {subject}'s pup up to the light. | {subject}'s pup cried through the whole blessing.
b[priest] : The loudest blessing I've done this {season}. A good sign.
b : A good start. | Lovely custom. | They all do.
a : The best. | It is. | Every one.

scene et_bi_guard
when topic=life kind=birth b.role=guard
a : Did you hear about {subject}'s pup? | {subject}'s a parent now. | New pup on your round, {b}. {subject}'s.
b : I heard it from the street. Howling like a siren. | Good for them. | I'll keep the street quiet for them.
a : Strong lungs. | Isn't it. | Kind.

scene et_bi_rivals
when topic=life kind=birth band=rivals
a : {subject}'s pup. I suppose you'll be first to visit with some showy gift. | You'll not outdo my gift for {subject}'s pup. | I've already been to see {subject}'s pup. Before you.
b : A blanket. Not showy. | It's not a contest. | Congratulations. You win.
a : Hand-stitched, I'll bet. | It is now. | I know.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- Apprenticeships

scene et_ap_healer
when topic=life kind=apprentice b.job=healer
a : Heard {subject}'s apprenticed. Would you ever take one? | {subject}'s learning a trade. You should teach someone yours. | Who'll learn the herbs when you're gone?
b : When I find one who doesn't faint at blood. | One day. | That keeps me awake.
a : Hard to find. | Do. | Teach, then.

scene et_ap_hall
when topic=life kind=apprentice place=hall
a : {subject}'s indenture's been recorded at the hall. Official now. | The clerk read {subject}'s papers aloud. | Seven years for {subject}, signed and sealed.
b[official] : Three copies. One for the master, one for the hall, one for the family.
b : No backing out now. | Very grand. | Long road.
a : None. | Grand for a sweeping job. | Long and worth it.

scene et_ap_spouses
when topic=life kind=apprentice band=spouses
a : {subject}'s apprenticed. Should we think about ours? | Our pup's nearly old enough, like {subject}. | {subject} went at twelve. Is that too young?
b : Not yet. Let them be young. | Nearly. Not quite. | It's how we did it.
a : A while longer, then. | Not quite. | Was it good for us?

scene et_ap_old
when topic=life kind=apprentice a.stage=old
a : {subject}'s apprenticed. I was apprenticed sixty years ago. Feels like last week. | In my day, masters beat apprentices. I hope {subject}'s is kinder. | Good for {subject}. A trade is a life.
b : Sixty years! | Mine did. | It is.
a : Last week. | Kinder times. | A whole life.

scene et_ap_guard
when topic=life kind=apprentice a.role=guard
a : {subject}'s apprenticed. One less pup loitering at the gate. | Good. Idle pups make trouble. {subject}'s busy now. | I caught {subject} stealing apples last year. Now look. Apprentice.
b : Every bit helps. | You'd know. | People change.
a : It does. | I would. | Sometimes.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- Successions

scene et_su_hall
when topic=life kind=succession place=hall
a : The hall's acknowledged {subject} as head now. | {subject} sat in the old chair today. Looked very small in it. | {subject}'s name's on the hall roll now.
b[official] : All by the book. Nobody can contest it now.
b : Official, then. | They'll grow into it. | Lasting.
a : Official. | Or it'll swallow them. | For now.

scene et_su_worker
when topic=life kind=succession b.job=laborer|servant|crafter
a : You work for {subject} now. How is it? | Is {subject} a fair master? | Did {subject} keep you on?
b : Too early to tell. | Fairer than the old one. | For now.
a : Give it time. | That's something. | Good.

scene et_su_child
when topic=life kind=succession a.stage=young|adolescent
a : Is {subject} the boss now? | {subject}'s in charge now. Do they get a crown? | Why does {subject} get everything?
b : They are. | No crown. Just worries. | Because someone had to.
a : Can I be the boss? | Boring. | Lucky.

scene et_su_merchant
when topic=life kind=succession b.role=merchant
a : With {subject} in charge, will prices change? | {subject}'s taken over the shop. Are you worried? | Competitor or friend, {subject}?
b : Prices always change. | I'm always worried. | Depends what they sell.
a : Upward. | That's business. | Wise.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- Arrests

scene et_ar_barracks
when topic=crime kind=arrest place=barracks
a : They brought {subject} in last night. Kicking. | {subject}'s in the lock-up. Smells like they slept in a ditch. | Bunk duty's guarding {subject} tonight. Lucky me.
b[guard] : I've got the bite marks to prove it.
b : Kicking and shouting. | Probably did. | Bring a book.
a : Loud one. | Smelly one. | Can't read.

scene et_ar_chapel
when topic=crime kind=arrest place=chapel
a : They took {subject} right outside the chapel. After prayers. | {subject} came to confess, then the watch took them. | Did you see? {subject}, arrested on the chapel steps.
b[priest] : Confession is between a wolf and the Bright One. I said nothing to the watch.
b : After prayers! | Unlucky timing. | I saw. Everyone saw.
a : Bold of the watch. | Or guilty conscience. | Everyone.

scene et_ar_shore
when topic=crime kind=arrest place=shore
a : They pulled {subject} off a boat at the jetty. | {subject} tried to swim for it. Watch fished them out. | Arrested at the shore, {subject}. Trying to get away by water.
b[fisher] : Caught like a fish. Ha.
b : Nowhere to go. | Swim? In that cold? | Nearly made it.
a : Nowhere at all. | Desperate. | Nearly.

scene et_ar_gate
when topic=crime kind=arrest place=gate
a : They took {subject} at the gate, trying to leave. | {subject} nearly made it out. Gatekeeper stopped them. | Guards at the gate grabbed {subject} with a full pack.
b[guard] : Running with a full pack says enough.
b : Running, then. | Close one. | Packed for a long trip.
a : Guilty as sin. | Too close. | A very long one.

scene et_ar_rivals
when topic=crime kind=arrest band=rivals
a : Your friend {subject}'s arrested. Proud of them? | Didn't you used to drink with {subject}? | {subject} in the cells. You must be so ashamed.
b : They're no friend of mine. | Once. Years ago. | Not as ashamed as you'd like.
a : Not now, eh? | Interesting. | Shame.

scene et_ar_spouses
when topic=crime kind=arrest band=spouses
a : {subject}'s arrested. We had them to supper last month. | Do you think we should've seen it, with {subject}? | I'm glad they took {subject}. I always felt odd near them.
b : I'll count the spoons. | No one saw it. | You always had a nose for people.
a : Ha. | Nobody. | I do.

scene et_ar_old
when topic=crime kind=arrest a.stage=old
a : They've taken {subject}. I said so years ago. Nobody listened. | {subject}! I knew their grandfather. Honest wolf. | In my day, the stocks. Now it's cells and clerks.
b : You did say so. | Bad blood skips. | Clerks are crueller.
a : Every year. | It does. | Much.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- Newcomers, at the edges of town

scene et_nw2_gate
when topic=newcomer place=gate
a : {subject} came through the gate with a cart of everything they own. | Gatekeeper asked {subject}'s business. They said staying. | {subject} stood at the gate a long time, deciding.
b[guard] : I wrote them down. Name, trade, where from. They hadn't much of any.
b : All of it in one cart. | Staying. A brave word. | What did they decide?
a : Not much, then. | Very brave. | To come in.

scene et_nw2_field
when topic=newcomer place=field|work
a : {subject}'s come to work the fields. Strong arms. | New paws at the harvest. {subject}. | {subject} doesn't know a scythe from a spade.
b[farmer] : They'll learn by the third blister.
b : We need arms. | Every paw helps. | They'll learn.
a : We do. | It does. | Or lose a toe.

scene et_nw2_chapel
when topic=newcomer place=chapel
a : {subject} sat in the back pew this morning. Alone. | The newcomer {subject} lit a candle. For someone left behind, I'd guess. | {subject} came to chapel. Didn't know the hymns.
b[priest] : They know them now. I'll lend them a book.
b : Next time, sit with them. | Poor soul. | They'll learn them.
a : I will. | Poor soul. | Kind.

scene et_nw2_hall
when topic=newcomer place=hall
a : {subject} registered at the hall today. Officially one of us. | The clerk made {subject} spell their name three times. | {subject} asked the hall for a plot of land.
b[official] : Paperwork is how a town knows who belongs to it.
b : Welcome, then. | Clerks love that. | Bold.
a : Welcome. | They do. | Very bold.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- Festivals at the edges

scene et_fe2_shore
when topic=festival place=shore
a : Boats decked with flowers for {festival}. | They'll race skiffs across the water for {festival}. | Lanterns on the water at {festival}.
b[sailor] : I'll win the race this year. Watch me.
b : Pretty as anything. | My money's on the old fisher. | Like stars.
a : Pretty. | Always is. | Like stars.

scene et_fe2_barracks
when topic=festival place=barracks
a : Captain says half of us get leave for {festival}. | Polish your buckles for the {festival} parade. | Somebody's smuggled a cask in for {festival}.
b[soldier] : Half! Which half? Not mine, I'll wager.
b : Draw lots. | My buckles are spotless. | Don't tell the captain.
a : Lots. | Never. | Wouldn't dream of it.

scene et_fe2_chapel
when topic=festival place=chapel
a : The chapel's full of greenery for {festival}. | Candles on every ledge for {festival}. | Choir's practising for {festival}.
b[priest] : The Bright One loves a full chapel. So do I.
b : Smells of forest. | Lovely. | Badly.
a : It does. | Very. | They'll be ready.
